      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=20$, then what is the value of  $x?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =20 \\\\ (2+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =20 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =20 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{20}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =4 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, $x={e}^{4}.$",
